          "filename": "Eastern End of the Cursus - geograph.org.uk - 290297.jpg",
          "caption": "Eastern End of the Cursus This looks west along the length of the Cursus - it used to stretches beyond the line of trees on the horizon, where a gap has been deliberately left.  The odd lump and bump on the ground gives evidence of its existence - thought to be about 6000 years old, making it more ancient than nearby Stonehenge. Shaped like a long and thin racecourse, it was thought to be used for processions and parades.",
